  "strategy": "Define a new hybrid optimization method that combines projected gradient descent with a genetic algorithm and active-set Newton refinement to minimize the objective function. The central idea is to use gradient-based methods for local refinement while maintaining a diverse population through genetic operations, and finally to correct any stability issues with active-set Newton steps.\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - If parent sequence available, normalize it by dividing by its sum (ensuring sum >=0.01)\n   - If no parent, initialize with uniform random distribution (length=512)\n   - Set maximum iterations to 1800 (real in 768)\n   - Define population size (50) and genetic crossover parameters\n   - Compute initial objective for benchmarking\n\n2.Mathematical formulation:\n   - Optimization domain: probability simplex with sum=1, coefficients between 0 and 1000\n   - Objective function: minimize 2n * max(convolution) / (sum)^2\n   - Constraints preserved by projected gradient and simplex normalization\n   - Genetic crossover: blend between two parents with random mutation\n   - Active-set refinement: correction based on binding gradients from major peaks\n\n3.Implementation steps:\n   - Phase1 (Time: 200s): Projected gradient descent with momentum\n   - Phase2 (Time: 600s): Genetic mutation and crossover exploration\n   - Phase3 (Time: 1000s): Active-set Newton refinement in binding directions\n   - For each of these phases, perform iterative updates and breakpoints at 0.1 of time limits\n   - Track best sequence through exact evaluation\n\n4.Search choices:\n   - Population sizing controlled by time budget\n   - Genetic crossover probability based on iteration count\n   - Newton damping factor adapting to gradient stability\n   - Scheduled length adjustments from 512 to 1024\n\n5.Computational budget:\n   - Use Fast-Convolve (FFT-based) for evaluation\n   - Time allocated proportionally to expensive operations\n   - Check for local minima using cross-term comparisons\n\n6.Validation procedure:\n   - Maintain best sequence separately from algorithm candidates\n   - Evaluate fallback to uniform distribution\n   - Enforce non-negative values by clipping gradients\n   - Return sequence list after final checking",
